# 03: Alert Tuning, File Integrity Monitoring (FIM), and SCA Baselines

## Overview
After integrating Sysmon with Wazuh, the baseline ruleset often generates high-volume noise from legitimate Windows background processes. This guide covers tuning custom rules in `local_rules.xml`, configuring real-time File Integrity Monitoring (FIM), and reviewing Security Configuration Assessment (SCA) baselines on `Win-DC01`.

---

## 1. Tuning Noisy Processes in `local_rules.xml`
To reduce false positives without losing visibility into suspicious process creation (Sysmon Event ID 1), add child suppression rules that inherit from your base Sysmon rule (`100100`).

1. Open the Wazuh Manager rules file:
   ```bash
   sudo nano /var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml
   ```
2. Add child rules with `<level>0</level>` to silence known benign Windows binaries and services:
   ```xml
   <group name="sysmon,tuning,">
     <!-- Suppress routine Windows Update and Defender tasks -->
     <rule id="100101" level="0">
       <if_sid>100100</if_sid>
       <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)C:\\Windows\\System32\\(svchost|taskhostw|conhost|backgroundTaskHost)\.exe\$</field>
       <description>Sysmon - Suppress routine Windows background executables</description>
     </rule>

     <!-- Suppress Windows Defender signature updates -->
     <rule id="100102" level="0">
       <if_sid>100100</if_sid>
       <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)C:\\ProgramData\\Microsoft\\Windows Defender\\Platform\\.*\\MpCmdRun\.exe\$</field>
       <description>Sysmon - Suppress Windows Defender command line utility</description>
     </rule>
   </group>
   ```
3. Validate the rule syntax and restart the Wazuh Manager:
   ```bash
   sudo /var/ossec/bin/wazuh-analysisd -t
   sudo systemctl restart wazuh-manager
   ```

